============================================================
                    DEVELOPMENT PHASES
============================================================

PHASE 0 — FINAL ARCHITECTURE
------------------------------------------------------------
Freeze the architecture around the final product before coding.

Include:
  • Agent Runtime
  • Model Layer
  • Tool System
  • Safety Layer
  • Repository Engine
  • Git Engine
  • Reasoning Trace Store (.reason)


PHASE 1 — ENVIRONMENT & HARDWARE VALIDATION
------------------------------------------------------------
Check:
  • Python
  • Ollama
  • Git
  • CPU / RAM
  • GPU / VRAM
  • Disk space
  • Available DeepSeek models

PHASE 2 — OLLAMA / DEEPSEEK MODEL LAYER
------------------------------------------------------------
Build a configurable Python interface between the agent and
DeepSeek through Ollama.

Support:
  • Thinking output
  • Final response output
  • Tool calls

Save the thinking output from each model turn into .reason files.


PHASE 3 — AGENT RUNTIME
------------------------------------------------------------
Implement the core:
  User → Reason → Save .reason → Tool → Result → Reason → ...

Integrate reasoning trace persistence into the agent loop.


PHASE 4 — REPOSITORY DISCOVERY
------------------------------------------------------------
Scan repository structure, files, directories, project metadata,
and Git state.

PHASE 5 — TOOL SYSTEM
------------------------------------------------------------
Create the controlled tool registry and tool interfaces.

Initial tools:
  • read_file()
  • write_file()
  • edit_file()
  • search_repo()
  • run_command()
  • git_status()
  • git_diff()
  • git_commit()
  • git_push()

PHASE 6 — FILE READING
------------------------------------------------------------
Implement safe and efficient file reading.

PHASE 7 — REPOSITORY SEARCH
------------------------------------------------------------
Allow the agent to locate relevant files, functions, classes,
references, configuration, tests, and other code.

============================= MVP =============================

PHASE 8 — FILE EDITING
------------------------------------------------------------
Add controlled creation, modification, and patch-based editing.

PHASE 9 — COMMAND EXECUTION
------------------------------------------------------------
Allow approved commands, tests, builds, and scripts to run.

PHASE 10 — SAFETY & APPROVAL SYSTEM
------------------------------------------------------------
Require approval for risky operations and protect against
destructive commands and unintended repository changes.

PHASE 11 — GIT INTEGRATION
------------------------------------------------------------
Implement:
  • status
  • diff
  • log
  • branch
  • other required Git operations

PHASE 12 — SELF-REVIEW & TESTING LOOP
------------------------------------------------------------
Agent:
  Edit → Test → Analyze failure → Fix → Retest → Review diff

PHASE 13 — GIT COMMIT
------------------------------------------------------------
Review changes, generate a commit message, request approval,
and create the commit.

PHASE 14 — GIT PUSH
------------------------------------------------------------
Push approved commits to the configured GitHub remote.

============================= V1 ==============================

PHASE 15 — CONTEXT MANAGEMENT
------------------------------------------------------------
Select relevant repository context instead of sending the entire
repository to DeepSeek unnecessarily.

PHASE 16 — LARGE REPOSITORY SUPPORT
------------------------------------------------------------
Improve scanning, searching, indexing, and context handling for
large repositories.

PHASE 17 — FAILURE RECOVERY
------------------------------------------------------------
Handle:
  • Failed commands
  • Invalid edits
  • Timeouts
  • Missing files
  • Test failures
  • Git conflicts
  • Push failures
  • Invalid tool calls

PHASE 18 — PROJECT INSTRUCTIONS
------------------------------------------------------------
Support repository-specific rules such as AGENTS.md.

PHASE 19 — SESSION MANAGEMENT
------------------------------------------------------------
Maintain task state, tool history, decisions, changes, command
results, and reasoning trace references during an agent session.

PHASE 20 — EVALUATION FRAMEWORK
------------------------------------------------------------
Create repeatable coding tasks and measure:
  • Task success
  • Coding accuracy
  • Test success
  • Tool-call errors
  • Latency
  • Unnecessary actions

PHASE 21 — PERFORMANCE OPTIMIZATION
------------------------------------------------------------
Optimize:
  • Repository scanning
  • Context selection
  • File caching
  • Model loading
  • Tool execution
  • Search
  • Overall latency

PHASE 22 — MODEL BENCHMARKING
------------------------------------------------------------
Compare suitable DeepSeek model variants using the project's
actual coding-agent evaluation tasks.

============================= V2 ==============================

PHASE 23 — GITHUB API INTEGRATION
------------------------------------------------------------
Add GitHub-specific capabilities beyond local Git operations.

PHASE 24 — ISSUE & PULL REQUEST AUTOMATION
------------------------------------------------------------
Enable workflows such as:

  Issue → Inspect → Implement → Test → Commit → Push → PR


============================================================
                    FINAL SUCCESS CRITERION
============================================================

Give the agent:

  "Fix this problem in this unfamiliar repository."

It should be able to:

  1. Understand the repository
  2. Find the relevant code
  3. Plan the solution
  4. Save its reasoning trace to .reason
  5. Make the required changes
  6. Run appropriate tests
  7. Diagnose and fix failures
  8. Review its own diff
  9. Ask for approval
 10. Commit the changes
 11. Push them to GitHub


============================================================
                    ARCHITECTURE PRINCIPLE
============================================================

The MVP must NOT be a throwaway prototype.

Every MVP component should be designed as a smaller version of
the final system.

                    MVP
                     |
             add capabilities
                     |
                     v
                    V1
                     |
             add integrations
                     |
                     v
                    V2

Avoid unnecessary complexity early.

Do NOT begin with:
  • Vector databases
  • RAG
  • Multi-agent systems
  • Web UI
  • Cloud deployment
  • GitHub API
  • Complex persistent memory
  • Autonomous background processes

Add them only when the project demonstrates a real need.

